title: Repentance Is a Change of Mind – Turning to God
description: Biblical repentance is a change of mind toward God and belief of His word, not turning from sins to be saved.
output: lessons/doctrine/topic-salvation-repentance-change-of-mind.html
highlight: repent

# Built from the author's teachings under "Repentance is a change of mind" in studies/data/topics/salvation.json,
# with related teachings on the same theme (12062, 2015, 2293, 1680, 5437, 12092, 11662, 14979, 12454, 5456).
# The slide text is the author's own sentences, trimmed. The doctrine is kept exactly as taught.

=== The doctrine
# from 11405
--- Repentance Is a Change of Mind [title]
overline: Doctrine · Salvation
heading: Repentance Is a Change of Mind
sub: Repentance means a change of heart, to believe something that we hadn’t before.
refs: Acts 26:20; Acts 11:18; Hebrews 6:1
hint: Use the arrow keys or swipe to move through the study. Tap any gold reference to read the verse.

# from 1680, 3409
--- Repent and turn to God
kicker: The doctrine
big: Acts 26:20 | mark: repent and turn to God
lead: We must turn from false beliefs regarding righteousness, and turn to believing God’s report, obeying the Gospel, by trusting in Jesus Christ alone as the sole propitiation for our sins. This is what the Bible says **repent** means.
p: It is turning your trust from false beliefs, your own works, false gods and idols, and towards God. And once saved doing works suitable and proper for such a turning.

# from 12062, 805
--- Metanoia
lead: *Metanoia*, the Greek word translated in our King James Bible as “repentance,” is a changing of the mind.
p: “Meta” comes from the root for “after” or “beyond,” while “noeo” is “to think.” So metanoeo means to reconsider or to rethink something. At its root, repentance is about changing one’s mind.
p: That’s why in scripture, again and again, repentance and belief are used in tandem to express conversion, by believing on Jesus and receiving the gift of eternal life promised by God.
callout: Repentance is a changing of mind or turning from one thing to another.

=== The Bible defines itself
# from 2015, 2293, 805
--- God repented
big: Jonah 3:10 | mark: God saw their works, that they turned from their evil way; God repented
p: Here we get the biblical definition of repent, God changing His mind, AND the acknowledgement that “turning from their evil ways” ie “Repenting of sin” = works.
p: So in one verse you have evidence that repenting of sin is a work, and that repentance does not always mean of sin, because God does it.
note: No one would say God repented of his sin because God is sinless. The Bible defines itself.

# from 2839
--- Repentance unto life
kicker: Acts 11
scripture: Acts 11:18; Acts 11:21 | mark: granted repentance unto life; turned unto the Lord
p: Note that verse 18 and 21, both quoted above, state similar things. 18 using “repentance,” 21 “turned unto.”
callout: They have identical meaning. There is no implied “...from sin.” It is Biblical repentance, *turning to the Lord.*

# from 5415
--- That ye might believe him
kicker: Matthew 21
big: Matthew 21:32 | mark: repented not afterward, that ye might believe him
p: A certain man with two sons, orders them to work his vineyard. One refuses, but changes his mind and goes to work.
p: We see again, Biblical repentance is a turning towards belief in what God tells us is His will. Faith in His word.

# from 11662, 14979, 12062
--- Behave, or believe?
big: Matthew 4:17
panel-warn: “Behave” | Would He be telling Jews to clean up their acts …
panel: “Believe” | … or to believe they need salvation, and that that salvation, promised by God, was at hand?
p: John the Baptist is saying “Reconsider what you know and believe me.” He is not saying “get your life together and stop sinning, the Kingdom of Heaven is here.” [[Matthew 3:1-2]]

# from 6097
--- We go to the doctor to be healed
big: Luke 5:31-32 | mark: sinners to repentance
p: When Jesus says to repent, He isn’t talking about cleaning up your life, and trying to live more in line with the law. That’s the false religion of the Pharisee that He repeatedly condemns.
p: He calls sinners to repentance, towards God. The remission of sins that is the result of repentance unto faith. Then the healing of the body. Then the turning from sin.
callout: We go to the doctor to be healed. We don’t get healed to see the doctor.

=== What we repent of
# from 5444, 12092, 3072
--- I changed my mind
lead: I didn’t repent of sin to be saved because I’ve never thought sin was good or right. I’ve never changed my mind about that, I’ve always known it’s wrong.
p: But thank God and praise Jesus, I changed my mind about what I needed to do to be saved, and believed on the blood of the Lamb that washes away sins, and the gift of eternal life. That’s real repentance in order to be born again.
p: What people most often repent of are the false beliefs they have about God. About His judgment. About their own righteousness or how they can escape punishment for their sins.
tile: “ | Oh I thought idols would save me.
tile: “ | I thought the law would save me.
tile: “ | I thought maybe hell wasn’t real.
tile: “ | I didn’t know my works weren’t enough.

# from 5437, 2293, 11405
--- Turning from sin is a work
panel-warn: The false gospel | If you are trusting in your ability to turn over a new leaf, to be reformed from sin, even your subjective “willingness” to “repent of sin”, or anything else that you might do in order to be saved, other than believe, you’re perishing.
panel: The Bible | The phrase “repent of sin” doesn’t appear in scripture, nor does any similar implied doctrine regarding salvation. What we see over and over is belief, faith, that saved.
p: Everyone turns from sin at various points in their lives. None of that saves. Only faith in the risen Christ and belief in the promise of God that Jesus is the sole propitiation for all sin can save us.
refs: 1 John 3:4; Romans 3:20; Romans 3:28

# from 804, 5439
--- The verses in context
lead: It’s easy to understand, by looking at verses commonly cited as supporting the false damnable heresy of repentance of sin for salvation, to see precisely what is being repented of in each.
xr: 2 Chronicles 7:14 | Nation turning from sin. Not about salvation.
xr: Ezekiel 14:6 | Directed at the nation of Israel which had put their faith in idols.
xr: Luke 13:3 | They are as sinful as those they are judging; repent of that misunderstanding.
xr: Acts 3:19 | Repent of being ignorant of who Christ was that they and their leaders had killed.
xr: Acts 17:30 | Repent of the ignorance God had winked at: worshipping false gods.
xr: Revelation 9:20-21 | The unbelieving didn’t repent of their unbelief, to be saved physically from the wrath.
xr: Revelation 2:5 | Already saved church members, turn from being slack in doing the first works.

# from 2395, 804
--- Repent, and be baptized
kicker: Acts 2
scripture: Acts 2:36-38
p: Note the order of things. Peter gives them the Gospel. They ask what they now must do. Peter tells them repent, or change their mind. Not “of sin” but of what they, *in ignorance* and *unbelief* did, participating in His death. That they believe. Then they should be baptized in Jesus’ name.
note: They that received the good news and believed were then baptized. (Again note the order of operations.)

=== Sorrow is not the change of mind
# from 5612
--- Judas repented
lit: Judas repented himself | Matthew 27:3-5 | Repenting of a wrong done doesn’t equate with believing on Christ for salvation.
p: In modern translations, they remove the word “repented” here, and replace it with “felt remorse,” because the idea that Judas repented would conflict with the false teaching of turning from sin to be saved, rather than faith in Christ.
note: Bad doctrine begets bad doctrine and confusion. [[Acts 1:25]]

# from 5459, 1680, 5437
--- Godly sorrow and saving repentance
lead: It is the conflating of regret/godly sorrow/understanding of our sinful nature and the wages of death earned by it, with the actual change of mind, turning to faith in God, in Christ for our salvation, upon which bad doctrine and damnable heresies are founded.
panel: Recognizing we are sinners | Before we repent and believe, we have to first recognize we are sinners in need of salvation, damned without Christ. This is *not the same* as repentance, although it could be *the reason* for repenting.
panel: Godly sorrow | These may work us to the change of heart and towards faith, but they aren’t requirements for salvation, which is to believe on Jesus Christ, that He was raised from the dead. [[2 Corinthians 7:10]]

# from 12454
--- When he came to himself
kicker: Luke 15
scripture: Luke 15:17-20 | mark: when he came to himself; he arose, and came to his father
p: It was that turning towards the Father that shows repentance. Not his sorrow and regret. Not his willingness to work, or wanting to make his father his lord.
p: That is biblical repentance. It’s all God asks of us. That we turn to Him and allow Him to clothe us in righteousness that we did not earn or expect or deserve.

# from 2655, 5456
--- Believers who repented of a sin
fig: Simon | Acts 8:22 | Simon was already saved. He believed on Jesus already. He did something wicked, but his repentance isn’t for righteousness but as a believer who has sinned.
fig: The Corinthians | 2 Corinthians 7:9 | Paul isn’t telling the Corinthians that their turning from the sin he pointed out among them is how they were saved, for they were already.

=== What it breeds
# from 5460, 5461, 5459
--- More saved than others?
panel-warn: Resentment | The promotion of the idea that we must repent of our sins to be saved feeds that resentment, and can make a repentant heart grow bitter over time.
panel-warn: A caste | By placing the emphasis on what was turned from, rather than the testimony of what is turned to, it creates an almost inevitable form of a caste system within a church. Of respecters of persons, who in hypocrisy see themselves as *more saved* than others.
panel: The answer | We should not glory in the fact that those more sinful than we were saved. We should glory in God that he would save any of us. We were all equally damned. We all had to turn to believe the same thing to gain life.
note: There are many who are raised Christian, in godly homes, and can’t even tell you when they believed. [[Matthew 20:9-12]]

# from 11437, 5437, 2707, 5305
--- After salvation
lead: That discernment, if the new man created at salvation is fed, leads to a change in attitude towards sin. It becomes less desirable.
panel-warn: “Real repentance WILL mean you turn from sin” | Saying “real repentance WILL mean you turn from sin, and WILL walk in the Spirit,” is false. We must pick up our cross daily. It is a choice to engage in sanctification.
panel: Out of love for God | Having been saved, we still should turn from the sin that we did while perishing under the law. But this again isn’t repenting of sin to be saved. It is turning from sin out of love for God.
p: It is being born again, quickened in spirit, that leads us from sin. [[Romans 6:1-2]]

=== Summary
# from 1680, 2015, 2839, 5444, 5437, 2395, 12454, 3072
--- Summary
point: A change of mind | Repentance is a changing of mind or turning from one thing to another.
point: The Bible defines itself | Here we get the biblical definition of repent, God changing His mind.
point: Turned unto the Lord | They have identical meaning. There is no implied “...from sin.”
point: What we repent of | What people most often repent of are the false beliefs they have about God.
point: Sorrow may lead there | Not of sin, though godly sorrow for sin may lead us there. It was their faith that saves them.
point: Then turn from sin | It is turning from sin out of love for God.
take: If you have placed your trust in anything else besides the perfect work of Christ Jesus, this is what you must repent of.

